WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:03.637
- Ground reaction force
data, joint kinematics,

00:00:03.637 --> 00:00:07.730
underfoot pressure, thermal
cameras, high speed video.

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:10.550
The bridge between data and
performance is really that with

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:13.040
the right information we
can make better decisions.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.707
(intense music)

00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:29.820
Hi I'm Keith Stern and I
use data to help athletes

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:31.070
break records.

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:33.500
At the top level runners,
there's only a few seconds

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.380
between the person that finishes first

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:37.430
and the person that doesn't
podium finish at all.

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:39.880
So any small change can
really make a big impact

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:41.130
down the road.

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:43.740
My job here is to work
with the rest of the Reebok

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:47.455
Innovation Team to help bring
data into the design process.

00:00:47.455 --> 00:00:51.800
- Hi, my name's Dan Hobson,
I'm the VP of Innovation.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:55.060
There's a lot of information
to take into consideration.

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:58.870
On campus here, we built a
crossfit box and lived with

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:01.160
the athletes, monitoring
how they were working out,

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:03.360
what they were eating,
how they were recovering

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:06.180
and making that understandable
for the design team.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.230
Keith Stern is testing the product,

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:09.520
he's gathering data.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:13.557
The amount of data he can
compile with his team is vast.

00:01:13.557 --> 00:01:16.240
- A lot of our testing
on athletes starts here

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:18.350
in the human performance engineering lab.

00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:20.910
The force plate treadmill is
actually a split belt treadmill

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:23.776
so that we can record forces
from the left and right foot.

00:01:23.776 --> 00:01:26.443
(intense music)

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:30.640
The mechanical impacting
machine simulates what we see in

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.320
ground reaction force data and
then look at the performance

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:34.620
of the material over time.

00:01:36.768 --> 00:01:40.230
Through high speed video we
can track how much your foot

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:41.900
is moving in certain areas.

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:43.880
A lot of the times we can't
see with the naked eye

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:46.273
what's going on in those
really fast movements.

00:01:46.273 --> 00:01:49.720
So slowing it down, the transfer
of knowledge is way easier

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:51.760
than graphs and charts and numbers.

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:55.920
It's really easy to understand:
how the shoe works with

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.859
that person can make a big
difference in their running form.

00:01:58.859 --> 00:02:00.770
(footsteps)

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:03.110
Future of data collection
in athletic performance is

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:04.510
something that's really important.

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:07.700
There's a growing field of smart sensors,

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:10.170
sensors could be woven
into this technology that

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:12.070
we're developing with Flexweave.

00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:15.290
Flexweave is really a method
a weaving materials together

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:17.950
and allows us to combine
structure, durability,

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:20.430
into a single package that's
also still lightweight

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:21.410
and breathable.

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:23.150
The same types of things
that we record in the lab

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:25.850
we can then record on foot in the field.

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:29.460
- So if you have those smart
fibers in the Flexweave,

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:33.690
if you wanna track heart rate
or distance or put GPS in it

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:35.830
you know into those fibers
you can weave that into

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:37.000
the fabric.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:40.000
- The more data we have the
better we can make decisions on

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.330
what types of material
advances are actually making

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:43.750
an improvement.

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:47.090
Providing that performance
benefit, even if it's just comfort

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:49.410
to get people up and
active, that's what really

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:50.675
important to me.

00:02:50.675 --> 00:02:53.258
(gentle music)

